                <pb facs="0425" n="335" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg335"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862t.01/203: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Commissioner at Berlin</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dresel</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-11-28T14:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 28,
                                1920—2 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received November 29—2:50
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>1349. My No. 1342 of November 26.<anchor n="47" xml:id="d291-f1"/> The British
                    Ambassador here agrees with views expressed in my telegram and emphasizes <pb
                        facs="0426" n="336" xml:id="pg336"/> especially that he feels that the
                    speeches made in the occupied areas showed no intention of not executing treaty.
                    He is therefore recommending that his Government should not join in the French
                    protest but should make individual representations or present a joint note much
                    more moderate than the French one. This would not contain claim that
                    nonexecution of treaty was contemplated in the speeches but would point out the
                    great inadvisability of arousing public opinion by such utterances in the
                    Rhineland.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dresel</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
